#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = []
# ///

# vim: ft=python

# / Run OpenCode inside a Docker container.
# /
# / Usage: oc [options] [-- harness-args...]
# / Options:
# /   --profile <name>  Profile to run as. Selects the
# /                     `~/.config/opencode-<name>` directory, with its own
# /                     login, history, and credentials, and the `api_key`,
# /                     `model`, `agent`, and `mcp_servers` of that profile
# /                     in `~/config.json` (default: the organization
# /                     directory of the project, such as `orgname` for
# /                     `~/devel/orgname/project`, or the user name for a
# /                     project directly under `~/devel`)
# /   --channel <name>  OpenCode version to install in the image
# /                     (default: `latest`). Accepts `latest` or a version
# /                     such as `2.0.26`. The version is part of the image
# /                     tag, so each version gets its own image, and a
# /                     switch to another version builds that image. The
# /                     image of `latest` is rebuilt once a day. The image
# /                     of a version is not, because a version names one
# /                     release.
# /   --process-output  Capture and process output (requires a headless
# /                     `run` with `--format json` in harness args)
# /   --force-rebuild   Rebuild the Docker image even if it is up to date.
# /                     The build runs without cache, because a cached
# /                     build would install the same release again
# /   --no-rebuild      Skip rebuilding image even if outdated
# /   --allow-stale     If build fails, use existing image (fatal if none
# /                     exists)
# /   --no-docker-socket
# /                     Do not mount the host Docker socket into the
# /                     container
# /   --add-volume <path>[:rw] | <source>:<target>[:rw]
# /                     Mount an extra host directory into the container.
# /                     Repeat the option for each directory. A single path
# /                     uses the same value for source and target. The
# /                     source must be an absolute path to a real directory
# /                     that you own. Links resolve before the mount, and a
# /                     link as source fails. The target must be an absolute
# /                     container path outside of the fixed mounts, or `~`
# /                     or `~/...`, which resolves to the home directory
# /                     like on the host. Append `:rw` for read-write,
# /                     otherwise the mount is read-only.
# /   --verbose         Print the Docker command before running it
# /   --help, -h        Show this help
# /
# / Harness arguments:
# /   Without harness arguments, the launcher runs the interactive
# /   OpenCode interface from a fresh session with no tabs, with
# /   `--standalone` for a private server inside the container. Anything
# /   else runs headless through `run`: the launcher puts
# /   `run --standalone` before the arguments, plus `--model` with the
# /   `model` of the profile and `--agent` with the `agent` of the
# /   profile, if any. File tools may read or edit anywhere inside the
# /   container: the profile `opencode.json` allows
# /   every `external_directory` request, and the host trees the
# /   container sees read-write are `~/devel` and the read-write
# /   `--add-volume` mounts.
# /   A `--model`, `--agent`, `--standalone`, or `--server` that you pass
# /   wins. A first argument that names another subcommand (`serve`,
# /   `auth`, `mcp`, and the rest) passes through verbatim, except that
# /   `run` still gets `--standalone`, `--model`, and `--agent` as above.
# /   A prompt that starts with one of those words needs an explicit
# /   `run` first.
# /
# / Projects:
# /   A project is a directory that `list_project_dirs` reports. The
# /   current directory can be the project root or any directory below
# /   it. Both cases behave the same: the profile comes from the root,
# /   and the container starts in the root. If run from outside of
# /   `~/devel`, defaults to ~/devel/dotfiles as the project. A run from
# /   a directory under `~/devel` that is inside no project is an error.
# /   The default would start the agent on the dotfiles repository,
# /   where it can edit the scripts that run on the host, and a
# /   `.list_project_dirs_ignore` file asks for the opposite. `~/devel`,
# /   every project, and every directory between the two must be real
# /   directories, not links. The container sees `~/devel` through one
# /   mount, and a link whose target is outside of that mount does not
# /   resolve inside it. The launcher refuses to start when it finds
# /   one.
# /
# / Project script:
# /   When the project root holds a `jpenv-container.sh` file, the image
# /   build runs it at the end. The script runs as the container user, in
# /   the home directory of the image. The project is not visible to it.
# /   The script can run `sudo` without a password. The build removes
# /   that permission after the script, so the image does not allow
# /   `sudo`.
# /
# /   A project with a script gets its own image, named after the
# /   project. A change to the script rebuilds that image, and the daily
# /   rebuild of the channel applies to it as well.
# /
# /   The file can be a link, such as one into another repository under
# /   `~/devel`. The target must be a regular file under `~/devel` that
# /   you own. The image holds the content of the target, and the
# /   container must not see a file of the host that no mount exposes.
# /   The launcher refuses to start when the file breaks one of these
# /   rules.
# /
# / Configuration:
# /   Reads an optional configuration file at `~/config.json` with API
# /   keys and other configuration, through `jc get`. See `jc --help`
# /   for the rules on the file. The profile directory
# /   `~/.config/opencode-<name>` must be a real directory, not a link.
# /   The launcher refuses to start when it is one.
# /
# /   Configuration format:
# /   ```
# /   {
# /     "opencode": {
# /       "profiles": {
# /         "username": {
# /           "api_key": "ok-...",
# /           "model": "anthropic/claude-opus-4-6",
# /           "agent": "build",
# /           "mcp_servers": {
# /             "server-name": {"type": "local", "command": ["..."]}
# /           }
# /         },
# /         "orgname": {
# /           "api_key": "ok-..."
# /         }
# /       }
# /     }
# /   }
# /   ```
# /
# /   Every key is optional. A profile without an entry in `profiles`
# /   is a supported configuration. A detected profile without one
# /   only warns, and only when the configuration file exists. An
# /   explicit `--profile` without one is an error
# /   when the `~/.config/opencode-<name>` directory does not exist
# /   yet, because that is almost always a typo. To start a new profile
# /   without an entry, create the directory first.
# /
# /   One key pays for the session. `api_key` is an OpenCode (Zen) key:
# /   it funds models of the `opencode` provider through the Zen proxy.
# /   Without it only the free Zen models are enabled. With `model`, the
# /   launcher passes it to `run` with `--model` and writes it into the
# /   profile `opencode.json`, so the interactive interface uses it too.
# /   Give the full reference in `provider/model#variant` form, such as
# /   `anthropic/claude-opus-4-6#xhigh`. `agent` works the same way, with
# /   `--agent` and `default_agent`. Without `mcp_servers`, the profile
# /   keeps the `mcp.servers` object of the origin file.
# /
# /   The `mcp_servers` object of a profile replaces the `mcp.servers`
# /   object in the profile `opencode.json`. The profile `AGENTS.md`
# /   loads as the global instructions file. Project configurations stay
# /   disabled (`OPENCODE_CONFIG_PROJECT_DISABLE=1`), so the profile is
# /   the only configuration source, like `--setting-sources=user` for
# /   Claude Code.
# /
# /   An `OPENCODE_API_KEY` variable in the environment takes precedence
# /   over the `api_key` of the profile. The launcher names the source of
# /   the key when it comes from the environment, and warns when both are
# /   present, because the account of the environment key pays for the
# /   session.
# /
# / Environment:
# /   The launcher reads these variables of the host:
# /
# /   OPENCODE_API_KEY   The API key of the session. See `Configuration`.
# /   AGENTS_ADDITIONAL_ENV_VARS
# /                      A colon-separated list of variable names to relay
# /                      into the container with the value of the host. A
# /                      name that the launcher or the image sets for the
# /                      container is skipped with a warning.
# /   RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH
# /                      The options file of ripgrep. Relayed when the
# /                      file is under `~/devel`, where the container can
# /                      read it. Otherwise the container does not get it,
# /                      and a warning says so.
# /   DOCKER_HOST        The daemon of the host. A Unix socket is mounted
# /                      into the container. See `Security`.
# /   TZ                 The time zone of the container. Without it, the
# /                      launcher reads the zone from `/etc/localtime` or
# /                      `/etc/timezone`, and leaves it unset when neither
# /                      names one.
# /   TMUX_PANE          The pane that runs the launcher. See `tmux`.
# /
# / Security:
# /   The container runs read-only, with an explicit list of mounts. This
# /   limits mistakes. It is not a security boundary.
# /
# /   By default the host Docker socket is mounted and the container
# /   joins the group of the socket file, and on macOS also group 0,
# /   which owns the socket inside the Docker Desktop virtual machine.
# /   Any process in the container can then start a privileged container
# /   and take control of the host. Use `--no-docker-socket` for
# /   repositories that you do not trust. Docker is then unavailable
# /   inside the container.
# /
# /   The dotfiles repository holds the scripts that the agent calls and
# /   the `.opencode-origin` directory that every run copies into the
# /   profile. When the project is not the dotfiles repository, the
# /   container sees that repository read-only, so the agent cannot
# /   change the origin. The launcher warns when `~/.opencode-origin`
# /   resolves into `~/devel` but outside of that repository, because
# /   the `~/devel` mount is read-write there. An origin outside of
# /   `~/devel` is not visible in the container. The copy overwrites
# /   the files that the origin holds, and it mirrors
# /   the directories that the origin holds, so a file that the agent
# /   adds to `skills` does not survive the next run. A top-level entry
# /   that an earlier run installed and that the origin no longer holds
# /   is removed. The list of installed entries lives in
# /   `~/.oc-launcher/<profile>/manifest`, which no mount exposes, so
# /   the agent cannot make a run remove another entry. The lock that
# /   makes two launches of a profile take turns lives next to it, so
# /   the agent cannot stall a launch from inside the container. The
# /   rest of the profile directory is not touched. The `learn` skill
# /   writes to the origin, so it works only from the dotfiles project.
# /
# /   The container is limited to a number of processes and an amount of
# /   memory, so a runaway subprocess or a browser cannot take the host
# /   down. The limits are the `CONTAINER_PIDS_LIMIT` and
# /   `CONTAINER_MEMORY_LIMIT` constants in this script.
# /
# /   The mirror applies to every directory in the origin. `cli.json` in
# /   the origin sets the interface keybinds; edits made inside the
# /   interface itself do not survive the next run, like every other
# /   origin file.
# /
# /   When the project is the dotfiles repository, the agent can edit the
# /   scripts that run on the host, such as `bin/oc`, `bin/jc`,
# /   `bin/list_project_dirs`, and `bin/pane-state-sync`. The next launch
# /   runs that code as you. `--no-docker-socket` does not close this
# /   path. Run an agent on the dotfiles repository only when you trust
# /   it.
# /
# /   The `~/devel` mount is read-write, and file tools may reach all of
# /   it, so the agent can edit every repository under `~/devel`, not
# /   only the project. Only the dotfiles repository is read-only. Move
# /   a repository that the agent must not touch out of `~/devel`. Each
# /   `--add-volume` mount exposes one more host directory to the agent.
# /   The mount is read-only unless it ends in `:rw`. The launcher
# /   mounts the resolved source. It refuses a source that is a link. It
# /   refuses a source that is not a directory that you own. It refuses
# /   a target that overlaps a fixed mount or a system path. It refuses
# /   a source that exposes the launcher state. It refuses a read-write
# /   source that exposes the dotfiles repository outside of its
# /   read-only mount.
# /
# /   The image build runs the `jpenv-container.sh` file of the project.
# /   A cloned repository can commit one, and the agent can write one
# /   into any project under `~/devel`. The script cannot reach the
# /   host, but it controls the image that the agent of that project
# /   runs in. `--no-docker-socket` does not change that. Read the file
# /   before you launch on a repository that you do not trust.
# /
# /   The host directory `/tmp/oc-<user>/<profile>` is mounted at the
# /   same path in every container of the profile, so these containers
# /   share it. It holds the data, state, and cache directories of the
# /   tools in the image, which the `XDG_DATA_HOME`, `XDG_STATE_HOME`,
# /   `XDG_CACHE_HOME`, and `npm_config_cache` variables name, and the
# /   directory that the `AGENT_SHARED_DIR` variable names, where the
# /   hook scripts write. Only you can read those. The hooks see tool
# /   input and output, which can hold secrets from the session.
# /
# / tmux:
# /   The `pane-state` and `chime` scripts write below
# /   `/tmp/tmux-pane-states-<user>`, and `pane-state-sync` reads there.
# /   Each user of the machine has a private directory, named after the
# /   user, right below `/tmp`. Two users can have a pane with the same
# /   tmux target, so the states of one user must not reach the tmux
# /   server of another. The directory has no shared parent other than
# /   `/tmp`, whose owner is `root`, so no other user can rename or
# /   replace it. The container always gets the directory of the user at
# /   the same path, and the `TMUX_PANE_STATES_DIR` variable names it.
# /   Every profile uses that one directory, so a pane has one state
# /   file. When the launcher runs inside tmux, the container also gets
# /   the `TMUX_PANE` variable. The `plugins/pane-state.js` plugin calls
# /   the `running`, `ready`, and `default` scripts to mark the pane
# /   that runs the agent, like the `hooks`
# /   in `.claude-origin/settings.json` do for Claude Code, and `ready`
# /   requests the notification chime. The `pane-state-sync` script on
# /   the host picks both up. Outside tmux the variable is absent, and
# /   these scripts do nothing. A reaper clears the pane to `default`
# /   when the container exits, whatever the ending is.

